-- The field energy of charges q on the network is the least flux energy obeying Gauss's law, qᵀL⁺q
-- (Thomson). On a closed network Gauss's law forces the total charge to zero, since every line's flux
-- leaves one vertex and enters another. For a neutral q, writing L⁺ab = (L⁺aa + L⁺bb − R(a,b))/2 makes the
-- diagonal terms sum to zero, and the energy is −Σ over pairs a<b of q_a q_b R(a,b): a pair of unlike
-- charges adds its separation, a pair of like charges subtracts it. Every R(a,b) has the one denominator
-- τ(G), the network's spanning trees, so the energy is a difference of two sums of tree counts over τ(G):
-- the trees of the network with a and b identified, none when a and b are one vertex.
pair-trees : Nat → List FaradayLine → Nat → Nat → Nat
pair-trees n ls a b = if eqb a b then zero else kirchhoff (drop-vertex b (vertices-upto n)) (identify-ends a b ls)
